<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrew Jackson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1845">1845</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrew Jackson was an American general and the seventh President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1829 to 1837. He won fame by defeating the British at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> in 1815 and invaded Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-seminole-war">First Seminole War</a>. As president he signed the Indian Removal Act of 1830, which forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>, Seminole and other nations west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson.jpg" alt="Andrew Jackson" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ralph Eleaser Whiteside Earl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrew_jackson_head_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Creek War some 500 Cherokees fought beside <b>Andrew Jackson</b> against the Red Stick Creeks at the Battle of Horseshoe Bend in March 1814, John Ross among them, a service that brought them no protection when Jackson later pressed for removal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Only seven nations recognised it: the United States on March 3, 1837, when <b>Andrew Jackson</b> appointed Alcée La Branche chargé d'affaires; France on September 25, 1839, whose Legation of 1841 still stands in Austin; Belgium, the Netherlands, Denmark, which exchanged recognition for free trade, the Republic of Yucatán,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In McCulloch v. Maryland in 1819 the Supreme Court read the federal government’s powers broadly, and in 1832 South Carolina claimed the right to nullify a federal tariff, backing down when Congress lowered the tariff and President <b>Andrew Jackson</b> threatened force.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Andrew Jackson</b>, elected in 1828, vetoed more bills than all his predecessors together and claimed to speak for the whole people; Abraham Lincoln stretched the war powers to fight the American Civil War; and in 1868 the House impeached Andrew Johnson, whom the Senate acquitted by a single…</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>News of the treaty took a month to reach the United States, and in the meantime American forces under <b>Andrew Jackson</b> won the Battle of New Orleans on 8 January 1815.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The destruction of the schooner USS Carolina ended Commodore Daniel Patterson's makeshift fleet, whose delaying actions contributed to <b>Andrew Jackson</b>'s victory over the British at New Orleans in 1815.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-27">27 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Waxhaws, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in The Hermitage, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.93,-80.75&amp;year=1817" aria-label="Waxhaws on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2503 900 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Andrew Jackson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2953 1170h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2953 1170h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.93,-80.75&amp;year=1817">What was here in 1817? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is to be regretted that the rich and powerful too often bend the acts of government to their selfish purposes. Distinctions in society will always exist under every just government.&#8221;</p><footer>Veto message on the Bank of the United States, 1832 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/ajveto01.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There are no necessary evils in government. Its evils exist only in its abuses.&#8221;</p><footer>Veto message on the Bank of the United States, 1832 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/ajveto01.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Their object is disunion; but be not deceived by names; disunion, by armed force, is Treason.&#8221;</p><footer>Proclamation to the people of South Carolina on nullification, 1832 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Proclamation_43" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
